WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.706 --> 00:00:08.666
Attention Deficit Hyperactivity&nbsp;
Disorder, or ADHD, is mostly of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.666 --> 00:00:15.106
genetic origin. Symptoms are categorized as&nbsp;
inattentive or hyperactive, but people can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.106 --> 00:00:23.306
experience both in all sorts of forms — which&nbsp;
means there is a spectrum of presentations.

00:00:23.306 --> 00:00:29.226
Lisa, a young girl who wants to become a writer,&nbsp;
will help us understand what it means to grow up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.226 --> 00:00:38.786
with ADHD. Her story shows how her atypical&nbsp;
brain makes it harder to achieve her dreams.

00:00:38.786 --> 00:00:43.946
During early childhood, Lisa has a&nbsp;
tough time paying attention. She gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.946 --> 00:00:49.826
distracted, starts daydreaming, and&nbsp;
frequently forgets or loses things.

00:00:49.826 --> 00:00:54.066
Because her senses pick up too much&nbsp;
information for her brain to process,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.066 --> 00:01:00.466
she gets overwhelmed and as a result, she&nbsp;
often cries or becomes frustrated. Her parents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.466 --> 00:01:07.466
who don’t know better, scold her instead&nbsp;
of helping her deal with the pain.

00:01:07.466 --> 00:01:13.866
In primary school, Lisa can’t sit still&nbsp;
and often disrupts the class. She is smart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.866 --> 00:01:19.586
but besides English and the arts, has&nbsp;
bad grades. It is not long before Lisa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.586 --> 00:01:24.826
is branded as lazy — as someone&nbsp;
who doesn't use her potential.

00:01:24.826 --> 00:01:30.506
It’s also hard for Lisa to make friends.&nbsp;
She feels different and is bullied. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.506 --> 00:01:37.306
few friends she has tell her that she is&nbsp;
too emotional, but admire her enthusiasm.

00:01:37.306 --> 00:01:42.986
In middle school, Lisa starts doodling to help&nbsp;
her stay focused but her teacher doesn’t like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.986 --> 00:01:48.546
that and scolds her, because he thinks&nbsp;
she is distracted. And so she masks her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.546 --> 00:01:54.466
inattention and puts all her energy&nbsp;
into nodding and making eye contact.

00:01:54.466 --> 00:02:01.146
But by doing so, she loses her concentration&nbsp;
entirely and ends up not listening at all.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.146 --> 00:02:08.066
This is where she turns inwards and&nbsp;
develops a passion for creative writing.

00:02:08.066 --> 00:02:12.786
During high school, her mind starts&nbsp;
racing — with a thousand thoughts in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.786 --> 00:02:17.706
her head. She now wears headphones,&nbsp;
to isolate the noise and calm herself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.706 --> 00:02:22.626
down. But when she has her period&nbsp;
even that doesn’t help anymore and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.626 --> 00:02:29.426
she goes through intense mood swings. She&nbsp;
begins binge eating and starts smoking.

00:02:29.426 --> 00:02:36.146
At age 19, Lisa gets into a top literature&nbsp;
program. But the responsibilities of everyday&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.146 --> 00:02:44.186
life are overwhelming. She feels burned out,&nbsp;
stops showering, cleaning up, and going to class.

00:02:44.186 --> 00:02:50.306
One evening, she tells her best friend that&nbsp;
she can’t handle it anymore. All her life she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.306 --> 00:02:56.386
has been too different to ever fit in. All&nbsp;
her life she felt like a burden to others.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.386 --> 00:03:03.106
Her friend tells her about a psychiatrist,&nbsp;
and as a last resort, Lisa goes to see her.

00:03:03.106 --> 00:03:06.786
The psychiatrist first thinks&nbsp;
it's depression. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.786 --> 00:03:12.146
asking about Lisa’s childhood and family&nbsp;
history, she notices something else and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.146 --> 00:03:19.986
requests more tests. A few weeks later,&nbsp;
Lisa is diagnosed with ADHD and depression.

00:03:19.986 --> 00:03:26.306
The doctor explains to Lisa that ADHD is not&nbsp;
limited to the most well-known symptoms. For&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.306 --> 00:03:33.466
example, people with ADHD also have deficits in&nbsp;
their executive functions, such as working memory,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.466 --> 00:03:41.146
time perception, or emotional regulation.&nbsp;
Some also get sleepy when drinking coffee!

00:03:41.146 --> 00:03:46.266
However, they often also have higher&nbsp;
levels of creativity, are more caring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.266 --> 00:03:53.466
and are more curious than their peers, making them&nbsp;
great artists or scientists. Plus, they react more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.466 --> 00:04:01.426
calmly to stressful situations — some become&nbsp;
fearless firefighters or outstanding surgeons.

00:04:01.426 --> 00:04:06.066
The psychiatrist goes on to explain&nbsp;
that ADHD symptoms can be treated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.066 --> 00:04:12.546
with medication and therapy and that&nbsp;
regular sleep and exercise can help too.

00:04:12.546 --> 00:04:15.426
When Lisa takes her first pill the next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.426 --> 00:04:22.066
she bursts into tears. For the first time&nbsp;
in her life, her mind is quiet. It does not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.066 --> 00:04:27.866
take an effort for her to get up and shower.&nbsp;
She realizes that, throughout her whole life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.866 --> 00:04:34.306
she has spent so much energy trying to do&nbsp;
things that others do without even thinking.

00:04:34.306 --> 00:04:40.106
Although medication helps, Lisa knows that&nbsp;
it is not enough. She has to make changes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.106 --> 00:04:45.906
to her life and find work that plays to&nbsp;
her strengths. By the time she is 30,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.906 --> 00:04:50.026
she learns to accept and&nbsp;
appreciate her neurodivergence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.026 --> 00:04:57.506
and her atypical mind is celebrated&nbsp;
after the publication of her first novel.

00:04:57.506 --> 00:05:03.426
What do you think? If you had - or have-&nbsp;
ADHD would you adapt your brain to your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.426 --> 00:05:10.186
life by taking pills, going to therapy, and&nbsp;
masking your behaviors? Or is the solution to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.186 --> 00:05:34.186
accept your differences and adopt a lifestyle that&nbsp;
fits who you are? Tell us in the comments below!

